>> Pursuant to the recent discussion, the BPFK has been meeting to
>> decide what to do about declaring a very specific, offical Lojban
>> orthography that includes the best of various proposals extant, a
>> standard of marking stress that everyone can agree on, and the
>> flexibility to deal with alternative modes of typography in Latin
>> script.
>>
>> To that end, we've settled on the following general principles. =A0See
>> below for a specific example (using the Alice translation,
>> naturally).
>>
>> 1. =A0It has always been a founding principle of Lojban that it should
>> be easy to use with computers (this is what the ASCII convention was
>> about). =A0Taking this further leads to two minor changes:
>>
>> =A0a. =A0A new set of letters for marking stress has been chosen, that
>> =A0is at least as easy to use as capitals, but leaves capitals free
>> =A0for other orthographic uses without conflict. =A0For the most part,
>> =A0these letters were taken from current standard internet practice,
>> =A0however stressed "u" and "y" have no such standard, so they were
>> =A0picked to resemble similar practice, but have no weight of long
>> =A0use to back them up, unfortunately.
>>
>> =A0b. =A0The letter ' is now deprecated, since it routinely
>> =A0upsets computers (try dealing with a file named "lo'i.mp3" in a
>> =A0shell script some time).
>>
>> 2. =A0While periods are preferred, it is always allowed to drop
>> periods, as before, but if you do so, it is preferred that you
>> replace the following letter with its capital form, to enhance
>> visual distinctiveness. =A0For cases where a period has been dropped,
>> and the next letter is a stress letter, it's preferred to use both,
>> marking the text in some way to indicate that both are intended to
>> occupy the same space. =A0Details of this markup are left to usage to
>> decide.
>>
>> 3. =A0It is preferred to always mark stress, especially since we have
>> a much more convenient way to do so now; why leave out information
>> if you don't have to? =A0This includes cmavo.
>>
>> 4. =A0Compound cmavo are no longer allowed, at all. =A0Their use will
>> result in several large men arriving at your house in an unmarked
>> black car.
>>
>> 5. =A0For obvious reasons, using numerals for "p4", "r3", "c1", and so
>> on is deprecated.
